---
import BaseHead from '../components/BaseHead.astro';
import Header from '../components/Header.astro';
import Footer from '../components/Footer.astro';
import { SITE_TITLE } from '../consts';

const tabs = [
	{ id: 'software', label: 'software' },
	{ id: 'podcasts', label: 'podcasts' },
	{ id: 'blogs', label: 'blogs' },
	{ id: 'webpages', label: 'web pages' },
	{ id: 'lookbooks', label: 'shopping lookbooks' },
];
---

<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
	<head>
		<BaseHead
			title={`favorites — ${SITE_TITLE}`}
			description="Software, podcasts, and blogs Drew actually uses and recommends."
		/>
	</head>
	<body>
		<Header />
		<main>
			<article class="prose">
				<h1>My Favorite Things</h1>

				<p>
					This is a collection of my favorite tools, blogs, web pages, and podcasts =)
				</p>

				<div class="tabs" data-tabs>
					<div class="tablist" role="tablist" aria-label="Favorite things">
						{
							tabs.map((tab, i) => (
								<button
									role="tab"
									id={`tab-${tab.id}`}
									aria-controls={`panel-${tab.id}`}
									aria-selected={i === 0 ? 'true' : 'false'}
									tabindex={i === 0 ? undefined : -1}
								>
									{tab.label}
								</button>
							))
						}
					</div>

					<section
						class="panel"
						id="panel-software"
						role="tabpanel"
						aria-labelledby="tab-software"
					>
						<h2>Software</h2>

						<div class="entry">
							<h3>
								<img
									src="/images/favorites/gifox.png"
									alt=""
									width="28"
									height="28"
								/>
								<a href="https://gifox.app" rel="noopener">gifox</a>
							</h3>
							<p>
								Super easy and quick screen recorder that lets you create
								and edit gifs. Your PR reviewers will love you. I paid $15
								for a lifetime license. Amazing product.
							</p>
						</div>

						<div class="entry">
							<h3>
								<img
									src="/images/favorites/cleanshot.png"
									alt=""
									width="28"
									height="28"
								/>
								<a href="https://cleanshot.com" rel="noopener">CleanShot X</a>
							</h3>
							<p>
								This is just a great screen recording tool. I loved Loom prior to
								its acquisition by Atlassian, and I've tried to love Cap.so. But
								I've landed on cleanshot as the best tool for reliably recording demos.
								You might not need both gifox and cleanshot, but I bought the gifox
								lifetime license before I found cleanshot ¯\_(ツ)_/¯  
							</p>
						</div>

						<div class="entry">
							<h3>
								<img
									src="/images/favorites/tableplus.png"
									alt=""
									width="28"
									height="28"
								/>
								<a href="https://tableplus.com" rel="noopener">TablePlus</a>
							</h3>
							<p>
								If you're a developer, you need a first-rate db client, and I actually
								picked up TablePlus after Kao at Coverbase recommended it. It's a lovely
								client, and I've tried a lot of them over the years.
							</p>
						</div>

						<div class="entry">
							<h3>
								<img
									src="/images/favorites/meetingbar.png"
									alt=""
									width="28"
									height="28"
								/>
								<a href="https://meetingbar.app" rel="noopener">MeetingBar</a>
							</h3>
							<p>
								This app sits in your Mac OS menu bar and does 2 critical
								things:
							</p>
							<ol>
								<li>Tells you when your next meeting is</li>
								<li>Lets you join that meeting with 2 clicks</li>
							</ol>
							<p>
								I support this app through Patreon. It's made in Ukraine.
								What more do you need to know?
							</p>
						</div>

						<div class="entry">
							<h3>
								<img
									src="/images/favorites/runjs.png"
									alt=""
									width="28"
									height="28"
								/>
								<a href="https://runjs.app" rel="noopener">RunJS</a>
							</h3>
							<p>
								Just a nice standalone JavaScript repl. You can install
								packages. I find it real handy when I need to remember just
								how weird JavaScript is and want tight feedback loops.
							</p>
						</div>
					</section>

					<section
						class="panel"
						id="panel-podcasts"
						role="tabpanel"
						aria-labelledby="tab-podcasts"
					>
						<h2>Podcasts</h2>

						<div class="entry">
							<h3>
								<img
									src="/images/favorites/soft-skills.png"
									alt=""
									width="28"
									height="28"
								/>
								<a href="https://softskills.audio" rel="noopener"
									>Soft Skills Engineering</a
								> <span class="byline">by Jamison Dance and Dave Smith</span>
							</h3>
							<p>
								This is one of my favorite podcasts; Dave and Jamison are
								fun to listen to and they often have great advice for
								navigating the non-technical aspects of building software
							</p>
						</div>

						<div class="entry">
							<h3>
								<img
									src="/images/favorites/in-depth.png"
									alt=""
									width="28"
									height="28"
								/>
								<a href="https://review.firstround.com/podcast/" rel="noopener"
									>In Depth</a
								> <span class="byline">by First Round Review</span>
							</h3>
							<p>
								they interview folks who have worked in various roles at
								successful startups, and the conversations are always
								illuminating. I've quoted them elsewhere on my blog!
							</p>
						</div>

						<div class="entry">
							<h3>
								<img
									src="/images/favorites/joy-of-why.png"
									alt=""
									width="28"
									height="28"
								/>
								<a
									href="https://www.quantamagazine.org/tag/the-joy-of-why/"
									rel="noopener">The Joy of Why</a
								> <span class="byline">by Quanta Magazine</span>
							</h3>
							<p>
								they explore really fascinating and difficult subjects, and
								Steven and Janna are both excellent interviewers. My
								favorite episodes include the ones on graph theory, dark
								matter, string theory (which I previously found utterly
								inaccessible), and knots.
							</p>
						</div>

						<div class="entry">
							<h3>
								<img
									src="/images/favorites/switchblade-sisters.png"
									alt=""
									width="28"
									height="28"
								/>
								<a
									href="https://maximumfun.org/podcasts/switchblade-sisters/"
									rel="noopener">Switchblade Sisters</a
								> <span class="byline">by April Wolfe</span>
							</h3>
							<p>
								April is, IMO, the greatest podcast interviewer of all
								time. She is so prepared and knowledgeable, and she brings
								out the unique qualities and experience of each of her
								guests. Each episode is a very tight 45 minutes of insider
								baseball on the guest's favorite genre film — and the guest
								must be either an actor, writer, or director.
							</p>
						</div>

						<div class="entry">
							<h3>
								<img
									src="/images/favorites/lets-appreciate.png"
									alt=""
									width="28"
									height="28"
								/>
								<a href="https://kylascanlon.com" rel="noopener"
									>Let's Appreciate</a
								> <span class="byline">by Kyla Scanlon</span>
							</h3>
							<p>
								Kyla writes poetry about the economy and coined the term
								"vibe-cession". Anything she publishes is going to be her
								best work and well-worthy of your time. It's the rare
								podcast where I have to listen at a playback rate less
								than 1.
							</p>
						</div>
					</section>

					<section
						class="panel"
						id="panel-blogs"
						role="tabpanel"
						aria-labelledby="tab-blogs"
					>
						<h2>Blogs I like</h2>

						<ul class="blogroll">
							<li>
								<img
									src="/images/favorites/jvns.png"
									alt=""
									width="20"
									height="20"
								/>
								<span>
									<a href="https://jvns.ca" rel="noopener">Julia Evans</a> (be
									sure to read <a href="https://wizardzines.com" rel="noopener"
										>Wizard Zines</a
									>)
								</span>
							</li>
							<li>
								<img
									src="/images/favorites/chelsea-troy.png"
									alt=""
									width="20"
									height="20"
								/>
								<a href="https://chelseatroy.com" rel="noopener"
									>Chelsea Troy</a
								>
							</li>
							<li>
								<img
									src="/images/favorites/lethain.png"
									alt=""
									width="20"
									height="20"
								/>
								<span>
									<a href="https://lethain.com" rel="noopener"
										>irrational exuberance</a
									> by Will Larson
								</span>
							</li>
							<li>
								<img
									src="/images/favorites/charity-wtf.png"
									alt=""
									width="20"
									height="20"
								/>
								<span>
									<a
										href="https://charity.wtf/author/mipsytipsy/"
										rel="noopener">charity.wtf</a
									> by Charity Majors
								</span>
							</li>
							<li>
								<img
									src="/images/favorites/elided-branches.png"
									alt=""
									width="20"
									height="20"
								/>
								<span>
									<a href="https://www.elidedbranches.com" rel="noopener"
										>Elided Branches</a
									> by Camille Fournier
								</span>
							</li>
							<li>
								<span class="glyph" aria-hidden="true">//</span>
								<span>
									<a
										href="https://litonico.github.io/about-computers/"
										rel="noopener">About Computers</a
									> by Lito Nicolai
								</span>
							</li>
							<li>
								<img
									src="/images/favorites/computers-and-people.png"
									alt=""
									width="20"
									height="20"
								/>
								<span>
									<a href="https://computersandpeople.com" rel="noopener"
										>computers and people</a
									> by Martin Hertz
								</span>
							</li>
							<li>
								<img
									src="/images/favorites/dannorth.png"
									alt=""
									width="20"
									height="20"
								/>
								<a href="https://dannorth.net/blog" rel="noopener"
									>Dan North</a
								>
							</li>
							<li>
								<img
									src="/images/favorites/fromjason.png"
									alt=""
									width="20"
									height="20"
								/>
								<span>
									<a href="https://www.fromjason.xyz" rel="noopener"
										>From Jason</a
									> by Jason Velazquez (be sure to read <a
										href="https://www.fromjason.xyz/p/notebook/where-have-all-the-websites-gone/"
										rel="noopener">Where have all the websites gone?</a
									>)
								</span>
							</li>
							<li>
								<img
									src="/images/favorites/aphyr.png"
									alt=""
									width="20"
									height="20"
								/>
								<span>
									<a href="https://aphyr.com" rel="noopener">Aphyr</a> by Kyle
									Kingsbury (I loved <a
										href="https://aphyr.com/posts/341-hexing-the-technical-interview"
										rel="noopener">Hexing the technical interview</a
									>)
								</span>
							</li>
							<li>
								<img
									src="/images/favorites/zach-holman.png"
									alt=""
									width="20"
									height="20"
								/>
								<span>
									<a href="https://zachholman.com/about" rel="noopener"
										>Zach Holman</a
									> (especially <a
										href="https://zachholman.com/talk/utc-is-enough-for-everyone-right"
										rel="noopener">UTC is enough for everyone… right?</a
									>)
								</span>
							</li>
							<li>
								<img
									src="/images/favorites/pudding.png"
									alt=""
									width="20"
									height="20"
								/>
								<span>
									<a href="https://pudding.cool" rel="noopener">The Pudding</a>.
									I stumbled upon this site (specifically the <a
										href="https://github.com/the-pudding/wnba-politics"
										rel="noopener">wnba story</a
									>) on github's explore page because <a
										href="https://github.com/melodykramer"
										rel="noopener">Melody Kramer</a
									> starred it (I recommend following propublica journalists on
									github; they get up to some cool shit). Anyway the whole blog is
									incredible and it provides a bar I aspire to with my little
									dataviz experiments.
								</span>
							</li>
						</ul>
					</section>

					<section
						class="panel"
						id="panel-webpages"
						role="tabpanel"
						aria-labelledby="tab-webpages"
					>
						<h2>Web pages</h2>

						<div class="entry">
							<h3>
								<img
									src="/images/favorites/wikipedia.png"
									alt=""
									width="28"
									height="28"
								/>
								<a
									href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Time_in_Indiana"
									rel="noopener">Time in Indiana</a
								> <span class="byline">on Wikipedia</span>
							</h3>
							<p>
								There's a perennial debate about eliminating daylight savings time,
								and I learned from deep, personal experience that timezones are so
								much more complicated and political than you think they are. This
								wikipedia page is an excellent companion to Zach Holman's blog post.
							</p>
						</div>

						<div class="entry">
							<h3>
								<img
									src="/images/favorites/wikipedia.png"
									alt=""
									width="28"
									height="28"
								/>
								<a
									href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Anywhere_on_Earth"
									rel="noopener">Anywhere on Earth</a
								> <span class="byline">on Wikipedia</span>
							</h3>
							<p>
								There's a last place on Earth where any given day is still
								going — Howland and Baker Islands, out in the Pacific at
								UTC−12. "Anywhere on Earth" is the convention built on that: a
								deadline isn't blown until the date is gone everywhere, which
								lands at noon UTC the next day.
							</p>
						</div>

						<div class="entry">
							<h3>
								<span class="glyph" aria-hidden="true">&lt;&gt;</span>
								<a href="https://motherfuckingwebsite.com" rel="noopener"
									>Motherfucking Website</a
								> <span class="byline">by @thebarrytone</span>
							</h3>
							<p>
								The whole case for plain HTML, shouted, in 4.9 KB. It's a bit,
								but it's also just correct: semantic markup is responsive,
								accessible, and fast before you touch it, and everything you
								add after that is a choice you're making on someone else's
								behalf. Worth re-reading any time a framework starts to feel
								mandatory.
							</p>
						</div>

						<div class="entry">
							<h3>
								<img
									src="/images/favorites/ukiyoe-heroes.png"
									alt=""
									width="28"
									height="28"
								/>
								<a href="https://ukiyoeheroes.com" rel="noopener"
									>Ukiyo-e Heroes</a
								> <span class="byline">by Jed Henry and David Bull</span>
							</h3>
							<p>
								Video game characters rendered as traditional Japanese
								woodblock prints — Jed Henry draws them, David Bull carves and
								prints them by hand in a Tokyo workshop. The process is the
								part I keep coming back to: the same technique used for ukiyo-e
								two centuries ago, aimed at Link and Mario, one hand-carved
								block per color. The shop only opens for a few days at a time,
								so subscribe now and thank me later.
							</p>
						</div>
					</section>

					<section
						class="panel"
						id="panel-lookbooks"
						role="tabpanel"
						aria-labelledby="tab-lookbooks"
					>
						<h2>Shopping lookbooks</h2>

						<p>
							This is a collection of shopping lookbooks I've made, which is my
							term for a github pages website that claude builds for me via <a
								href="https://github.com/DrewHoo/shopping-lookbook"
								rel="noopener">this skill</a
							>. They're not really intended for a public audience (and their writing is quite AI-sloppy; I haven't revised it), but they are
							public repositories so why not index them, eh?
						</p>

						<div class="entry">
							<h3>
								<span class="glyph" aria-hidden="true">$</span>
								<a href="https://drewhoover.com/five-year-old-gift-lookbook/">Gifts for an almost-five-year-old</a>
								<time class="byline" datetime="2026-11-27">Nov 27, 2026</time>
							</h3>
							<a class="shot" href="https://drewhoover.com/five-year-old-gift-lookbook/" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true">
								<img
									src="/images/favorites/lookbooks/five-year-old-gift-lookbook.webp"
									alt=""
									width="1200"
									height="630"
									loading="lazy"
									decoding="async"
								/>
							</a>
							<p>One of my almost-five-year-old's favorite things to do is build things with masking tape, but it's hard for him to peel it off the roll himself. He also has and enjoys Magna-Tiles and Brio train tracks, and he often has the energy and disposition of a luchador. I wanted unexpected gift ideas, including some that have nothing to do with any of that.</p>
						</div>

						<div class="entry">
							<h3>
								<span class="glyph" aria-hidden="true">$</span>
								<a href="https://drewhoover.com/kettlebell-lookbook/">Kettlebell doubles at 16kg and 20kg</a>
								<time class="byline" datetime="2026-11-23">Nov 23, 2026</time>
							</h3>
							<a class="shot" href="https://drewhoover.com/kettlebell-lookbook/" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true">
								<img
									src="/images/favorites/lookbooks/kettlebell-lookbook.webp"
									alt=""
									width="1200"
									height="630"
									loading="lazy"
									decoding="async"
								/>
							</a>
							<p>My gold standard is a Kettlebell Kings hardstyle, which I own in 20kg: the handle is the right thickness and the finish is smooth but grippy. KBK seems to be out of business, my REP 16kg has a handle that's a little thin for me, and I want doubles at 16kg and 20kg, so I needed to know who else makes a bell like the KBK.</p>
						</div>

						<div class="entry">
							<h3>
								<span class="glyph" aria-hidden="true">$</span>
								<a href="https://drewhoover.com/exercise-mat-lookbook/">A mat for at-home workouts</a>
								<time class="byline" datetime="2026-11-05">Nov 5, 2026</time>
							</h3>
							<a class="shot" href="https://drewhoover.com/exercise-mat-lookbook/" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true">
								<img
									src="/images/favorites/lookbooks/exercise-mat-lookbook.webp"
									alt=""
									width="1200"
									height="630"
									loading="lazy"
									decoding="async"
								/>
							</a>
							<p>I've been using an old yoga mat that's probably 5mm thick and definitely not enough to keep my elbow from feeling the texture of my rental's vinyl flooring during planks. Time to upgrade! I'm using Rogue as a reference point.</p>
						</div>

						<div class="entry">
							<h3>
								<span class="glyph" aria-hidden="true">$</span>
								<a href="https://drewhoover.com/whey-isolate-lookbook/">Unflavored Whey Isolate Ranked by Contaminant Testing and Cost</a>
								<time class="byline" datetime="2026-10-26">Oct 26, 2026</time>
							</h3>
							<a class="shot" href="https://drewhoover.com/whey-isolate-lookbook/" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true">
								<img
									src="/images/favorites/lookbooks/whey-isolate-lookbook.webp"
									alt=""
									width="1200"
									height="630"
									loading="lazy"
									decoding="async"
								/>
							</a>
							<p>Turns out whey protein isolate is one of the best sources of protein you can get (look up leucine content and why it's important) and it at least doesn't upset my stomach. I learned this because I started a glp-1 this year and needed to really up my protein intake to avoid losing muscle. Anyway I also learned that protein sources can vary widely WRT contaminants; after I bought some WPI from bulksupplements(dot)com, I had claude email them to ask for their contaminant testing results and it turns out they wouldn't even detect lead until it hit 30x Prop 65's limit 😱 so I wanted to review all the WPI options from that lens.</p>
						</div>

						<div class="entry">
							<h3>
								<span class="glyph" aria-hidden="true">$</span>
								<a href="https://drewhoover.com/protein-shaker-lookbook/">Stainless Steel Protein Shakers Under $20, Dishwasher Safe</a>
								<time class="byline" datetime="2026-10-26">Oct 26, 2026</time>
							</h3>
							<a class="shot" href="https://drewhoover.com/protein-shaker-lookbook/" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true">
								<img
									src="/images/favorites/lookbooks/protein-shaker-lookbook.webp"
									alt=""
									width="1200"
									height="630"
									loading="lazy"
									decoding="async"
								/>
							</a>
							<p>Needed for said protein shakes. Turns out the little shaker balls cost like $1.25 apiece and work great in a mason jar with a lid. I never would've found that solution but claude did with this skill!</p>
						</div>

						<div class="entry">
							<h3>
								<span class="glyph" aria-hidden="true">$</span>
								<a href="https://drewhoover.com/kids-typing-lookbook/">Kids' Typing Lookbook: Touch Typing at 8, Mostly Free</a>
								<time class="byline" datetime="2026-10-17">Oct 17, 2026</time>
							</h3>
							<a class="shot" href="https://drewhoover.com/kids-typing-lookbook/" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true">
								<img
									src="/images/favorites/lookbooks/kids-typing-lookbook.webp"
									alt=""
									width="1200"
									height="630"
									loading="lazy"
									decoding="async"
								/>
							</a>
							<p>Middle child wanted to learn touch typing (which, bizarrely, they don't teach in public school?!), so I asked claude to research some good free programs.</p>
						</div>

						<div class="entry">
							<h3>
								<span class="glyph" aria-hidden="true">$</span>
								<a href="https://drewhoover.com/warm-desk-lookbook/">Warm Desk Lookbook: Heat the Person, Not the Room</a>
								<time class="byline" datetime="2026-10-11">Oct 11, 2026</time>
							</h3>
